WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.543
The best local model is not universal.

00:00:02.543 --> 00:00:06.730
Model choice should follow the task, the data sensitivity, the hardware available,

00:00:06.730 --> 00:00:10.219
the expected output format, and the level of review required.

00:00:10.219 --> 00:00:15.802
A model that is strong for brainstorming may not be the best choice for structured extraction,

00:00:15.802 --> 00:00:17.896
code assistance, or careful policy drafting.

00:00:17.896 --> 00:00:19.291
Hardware fit is central.

00:00:19.291 --> 00:00:23.130
Local model performance depends on available memory, accelerator support where present,

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:25.572
CPU speed, storage performance, and context length.

00:00:25.572 --> 00:00:28.713
If the model does not fit the machine well,

00:00:28.713 --> 00:00:33.249
the user experience may be slow, unstable, or too constrained for the workflow.

00:00:33.249 --> 00:00:37.436
Quantized models can make local use more practical by reducing memory needs.

00:00:37.436 --> 00:00:40.926
That tradeoff may change speed, output quality, or reasoning behavior.

00:00:40.926 --> 00:00:44.764
The right question is not whether quantization is good or bad.

00:00:44.764 --> 00:00:49.649
The question is whether the model configuration meets the workflow's quality and reliability needs.

00:00:49.649 --> 00:00:53.836
Smaller models can be excellent for drafts, routing, simple transformations, study help,

00:00:53.836 --> 00:00:54.883
and routine assistance.

00:00:54.883 --> 00:00:59.070
Larger models may help with deeper reasoning or more complex synthesis when

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:00.815
the local hardware supports them.

00:01:00.815 --> 00:01:03.955
Either way, the model should earn trust through testing.

00:01:03.955 --> 00:01:06.747
Evaluation should use the learner's own prompt sets.

00:01:06.747 --> 00:01:10.934
Try representative requests, compare outputs, note failures, and choose based on evidence.

00:01:10.934 --> 00:01:15.819
A local AI workflow is strongest when model selection is based on actual use,

00:01:15.819 --> 00:01:16.866
not reputation alone.

00:01:16.866 --> 00:01:20.006
Keep notes about why a model was accepted, rejected,

00:01:20.006 --> 00:01:22.100
or reserved for a narrow task.
